#chap-header("2", [Scene 1: The Investigation], "Following the trail northeast")

*Time budget: 30 minutes. Lesson: skill checks --- when to roll, what a
difficulty means, and how preparation pays off later.*

The party works the village for leads, then follows them into the field.
Two conversations and a short tracking challenge take them to the dump
site. That is the whole scene; resist the urge to let it sprawl.

#attention[
  *Teaching skill checks:* call for a roll only when the outcome is
  uncertain and failure has a consequence. If a player asks the reeve for
  the disappearance timeline and the reeve has no reason to hide it, just
  give it to them --- no roll. The checks below exist to reward digging
  deeper, not to gate the plot. Nothing in this scene can dead-end the
  adventure: the minimum the party needs (a direction to search) is given
  freely by Dashek.

  With seven players, spread the rolls around. If one player has rolled
  twice and another not at all, ask the quiet one what their character is
  doing.
]

== Lead 1: Reeve Ottavan

The reeve keeps the village records in a cramped office smelling of tallow
and ink. She is harried, worried, and practical --- she has been fielding
panicked families and shrinking search parties for weeks, and there is an
edge of resentment that it took dead _peacekeepers_ to get faction
attention.

- *What she provides freely:* the list of the missing --- names, dates,
  occupations, last known locations.

